    {
      "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50139584",
      "title": "Ask HN: Why people keep posting AI-generated projects around the Internet?",
      "source": "hackernews",
      "sourceName": "Hacker News",
      "summary": "Programming-related subreddits and other media platforms are current flooded by the waterfall of AI-generated projects. A typical post contains a problem description and the link to GitHub repo created just recently with sustainable amount of generated code in it. With the invite to review it, and to give a community feedback.<p>The quality of the projects vary, but very often these are codebases generated in a few days solely by the AI-agentic systems with little to no human review and prior history of development and recognition.<p>I&#x27;m asking this question not out of critiques. I genuinely don&#x27;t understand motivation and the roots of this phenomena.<p>Asking for the professional code review seems to be pointless for the publisher. Many of them didn&#x27;t have prior experience in the programming language in which the code has been generated. Furthermore, the code is seemingly not interested for the publisher either. It does not look to me as a good start for community-driven (F)OSS project anyway.<p>Another motivation that I can imaging is seeking for public recognition. But considering the amount of postings, I think it&#x27;s highly unlikely in the current situation even for the genuinely high-effort works.<p>I can also assume that some are seeking for the self promotion by faking professional skills with AI assistance. That might be true for some, but the majority don&#x27;t hide the fact that the presented code has been generated. It&#x27;s unlikely the main motivation too.<p>So, I completely lost in understanding on what&#x27;s going on, and what steams this never-stopping process. At a first glance the author doesn&#x27;t gain anything in return, and I don&#x27;t understand what they are seeking for.",
      "author": "Eliah_Lakhin",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-18T10:00:32Z",
      "engagement": 4,
      "tags": [
